Q: React-three/fiber canvas only displaying when I manually alter the viewport A: <p>The root cause of this behavior stems from how the React Three Fiber <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code> component calculates its internal dynamic viewport boundaries.</p>
<p>By design, the <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code> measures and inherits the runtime dimensions of its immediate parent DOM element. During the initial mounting and rendering phase, if the wrapping block-level container has no explicit CSS layout constraints (i.e., its height computed style evaluates to <code>0px</code>), the underlying Three.js WebGLRenderer instantiation collapses and defaults to the standard HTML5 canvas fallback dimensions of <code>300x150</code>.</p>
<p>When you manually alter the viewport, the browser dispatches a global <code>resize</code> event. R3F hooks into this event listener to trigger a structural window resize recalculation, causing the renderer bounds and aspect ratio matrix to dynamically snap to the updated parent container size.</p>
<h3>Resolution:</h3>
<p>To fix this architectural layout issue, you must encapsulate the <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code> component within a dedicated wrapper node and enforce rigid, explicit dimensional boundaries via CSS.</p>
<p>Here is the structurally corrected implementation using inline style properties to ensure instant viewport computation on the initial render:</p>
<pre><code>export default function App() {
  return (
    &lt;div style={{ width: &quot;100vw&quot;, height: &quot;100vh&quot;, position: &quot;relative&quot; }}&gt;
      &lt;Canvas camera={{ position: [0, 0, 5] }}&gt;
        &lt;ambientLight /&gt;
        &lt;Scene /&gt;
      &lt;/Canvas&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p><em>Note: If a full-screen viewport (<code>100vw</code>/<code>100vh</code>) is not desirable for your layout architecture, you can swap it out for standard relative/absolute units (e.g., <code>width: &quot;100%&quot;</code>, <code>height: &quot;500px&quot;</code>), provided the parent hierarchy ultimately resolves to a non-zero layout size upon the initial DOM parsing.</em></p>
